## 🎛️ Layer Breakdown
# 1. Presentation Layer
The system's frontline interface. It handles all network-level communications between external clients and the internal application ecosystem.

Responsibilities:

Exposing RESTful API endpoints.

Sanitizing inputs and performing syntactical data validations.

Mapping exceptions to semantic HTTP Status Codes (200 OK, 400 Bad Request, 404 Not Found).

# 2. Business Logic Layer
The operational heart of HBnB. This tier evaluates and executes the core business rules independent of how the data was received or how it will be stored.

Responsibilities:

Managing state mutations of domain models.

Enforcing data constraints (e.g., rating ranges, unique constraints).

Orchestrating domain entity behaviors through an atomic Facade pattern interface.

# 3. Persistence Layer
The data infrastructure tier. It handles the lifecycle of system records, managing memory or disk hard writes.

Responsibilities:

Abstracting raw CRUD operations through the Repository Pattern.

Isolating data persistence engines (SQL / NoSQL / In-Memory) from the business layer.

Ensuring transaction atomicity and safe record retrieval.

# 3. Business Logic Layer
## 📋 Review of Business Logic Requirements
To build a scalable and orderly system in HBnB, the business logic layer adheres to the following strict operational rules:

Unified Identity & Audit: Absolutely all entities in the system must have an immutable unique global identifier (UUID4), accompanied by automatic system timestamps that track the exact date and time of creation and latest modification.

Attribute Encapsulation: Sensitive or internal data must not be manipulated directly from the outside; instead, specific methods are exposed to safely mutate object state.

Domain Validation: Models are not empty data containers (Anemic Models). Each entity holds the responsibility to validate its own data before allowing it to save or update (e.g., ensuring a price is positive or a rating falls within the required range).

