# 5. Design Notes

## ⚖️ Architectural Trade-offs & Decisions:
The HBnB system design prioritizes maintenance simplicity and testing modularity over micro-optimization. The patterns applied ensure that business rules remain completely unaffected by infrastructural mutations.

## 🧩 1. Layered Architecture Clean Separation
By forcing strict single-direction dependencies (Presentation → Business → Persistence), we eliminate hidden side effects. Changes within database schemas cannot force compilation errors inside the presentation routing modules.

## 📐 Domain-Driven Object Design
Relying on rich domain models rather than an anemic model structure allows entities to protect their own data integrity. A Review object will refuse initialization if its rating constraint fails, preventing invalid data from spreading across layers.

## 🚪 Facade Design Pattern Implementation
The HBnBFacade class acts as the single gatekeeper for internal operations. This reduces the surface area of the Business Logic layer exposed to the Presentation layer. The API controllers never require knowledge about multiple interlocking services; they request a high-level command from the Facade, which orchestrates the sub-systems internally.

## 🗃️ Repository Abstraction Pattern
Repositories isolate database engines completely. The Business layer interacts with an interface declaration (save, get_by_id). This strategy guarantees that the system can operate on plain memory arrays (In-Memory Storage) during automated testing runs, and switch to a fully persistent relational engine (SQLAlchemy/PostgreSQL) in production via environment configuration switches.

## 🏁 Conclusion
This technical blueprint details a highly maintainable foundation for the HBnB Evolution application. The architectural decisions detailed here ensure that as feature sets expand—such as processing payment pathways or booking calendar allocations—the application can scale without requiring fundamental rewrites of its core data layers.
